# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "A loop that counts down"; slides "ICT3112 - 6 - Loops".
id: countdown
title: A loop that counts down
topic: Loops
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "A loop that counts down"; Slides 6, Loops
tags: while, invariant, variant, termination

task:
  Find a loop invariant, and use the loop rule with consequence to prove
  that the triple is valid. Then give a variant for the loop, and show that
  the loop terminates.

pre: x = N && N >= 0
post: x = 0

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while x > 0 do {
    {I && x > 0}
    {R1}
    x := x - 1
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(x > 0)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The invariant must hold each time the loop condition $x > 0$ is
  evaluated. From $x = N$ the variable $x$ takes the values $N$, $N - 1$,
  ..., $1$, $0$ there, and the loop stops at $0$. All these values are at
  least $0$.
set: I = x >= 0
text:
  The loop rule with consequence needs three checks, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $x > 0$: establishment $P ==> I$, preservation
  $\{I && B\}$ `x := x - 1` $\{I\}$, and exit $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: Establishment: P implies I
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  The invariant must hold when the loop is reached. No statement comes
  before the loop, so we prove that the precondition $P$ implies $I$.
  >>> «x = N && N >= 0 : P» ==> «x >= 0 : I»
vc: x = N && N >= 0 ==> x >= 0
why:
  Replace $x$ by $N$, using $x = N$. Then $x >= 0$ becomes $N >= 0$, which
  is part of $P$.

step: Preservation: R1, the weakest precondition of x := x - 1 for I
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: x := x - 1
text:
  We prove that one iteration keeps the invariant:
  >>> {«x >= 0 : I» && «x > 0 : B»} x := x - 1 {«x >= 0 : I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$. We substitute
  the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression ($x - 1$) in
  $I$.
calc:
  wp(x := x - 1, I)
  = (x >= 0)[x <- x - 1]
  = x - 1 >= 0      // x is replaced by x - 1
  = x >= 1          // add 1 to both sides
set: R1 = x >= 1

step: Preservation: I and B imply R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R1$,
  computed in the previous step.
  >>> «x >= 0 : I» && «x > 0 : B» ==> «x >= 1 : R1»
vc: x >= 0 && x > 0 ==> x >= 1
why:
  For integers, $x > 0$ is the same as $x >= 1$.

step: Exit: I and not B imply Q
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and the loop condition $B$ is false. This
  must imply the postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «x >= 0 : I» && «!(x > 0) : ¬B» ==> «x = 0 : Q»
vc: x >= 0 && !(x > 0) ==> x = 0
why:
  $!(x > 0)$ is $x <= 0$. Together with $x >= 0$ it gives $x = 0$.

step: Termination: the variant is not negative
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
text:
  The body decreases $x$ by one, and the loop runs only while $x > 0$. So
  we take the variant $E = x$. The rule WHILE-TOTAL has two checks. The
  first one: $E$ is not negative when an iteration starts.
  >>> «x >= 0 : I» && «x > 0 : B» ==> «x >= 0 : E >= 0»
vc: x >= 0 && x > 0 ==> x >= 0
why:
  $x >= 0$ is the invariant itself.

step: Termination: the body decreases the variant
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ASSIGN, WP
focus: x := x - 1
text:
  The second check: the body keeps $I$ and makes $E$ smaller. $E0$ is a
  logical variable for the value of $E$ when the iteration starts.
  >>> {«x >= 0 : I» && «x > 0 : B» && «x = E0 : E = E0»} x := x - 1 {«x >= 0 : I» && «x < E0 : E < E0»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for the
  postcondition of this triple. We substitute $x$ with $x - 1$.
calc:
  wp(x := x - 1, x >= 0 && x < E0)
  = (x >= 0 && x < E0)[x <- x - 1]
  = x - 1 >= 0 && x - 1 < E0     // x is replaced by x - 1
vc: x >= 0 && x > 0 && x = E0 ==> x - 1 >= 0 && x - 1 < E0
why:
  From $x > 0$ we get $x - 1 >= 0$. From $x = E0$ we get
  $x - 1 = E0 - 1 < E0$.
result: The triple is valid, and the loop terminates.
